vm-5 — Code generation: control / non-local exits / values (plan-2-bytecode-vm).

The remaining special operators — the ones with non-trivial control flow.

WHAT
- BLOCK / RETURN-FROM: lexical exit.  When the block is not captured by a closure,
  compile to a plain forward JUMP + a value slot (fast path).  When it may be
  invoked from an inner closure (escaping), fall back to a dynamic block frame
  (marker on the frame stack, RETURN-FROM unwinds to it) — mirrors the tree-walker
  frame model.  (Named-function implicit blocks are already lexical in the body,
  see [[bocl-defun-lexical-block]], so DEFUN needs nothing special.)
- TAGBODY / GO: intra-body jumps compile to JUMP for local tags; escaping GO uses a
  dynamic tag frame like BLOCK.
- CATCH / THROW: dynamic — push a catch frame (tag on the frame stack), THROW
  unwinds to the matching tag.  Reuse the C frame_catch machinery if practical.
- UNWIND-PROTECT: push a cleanup frame; any non-local exit through it runs the
  cleanup thunk.
- MULTIPLE-VALUE-CALL / -PROG1 / -BIND / VALUES / NTH-VALUE: use the VALUES opcode +
  the MultipleValues path already in the kernel.
- PROGV (dynamic bindings from a list), LOAD-TIME-VALUE (evaluate at load, store in
  the constant pool — ties to vm-8), EVAL-WHEN (compile the :execute body; the
  :compile-toplevel handling stays in COMPILE-FILE), FLET/LABELS (compile local
  functions as sub-templates + closures), MACROLET/SYMBOL-MACROLET (already expanded
  by vm-3).

ACCEPTANCE: compile+run RETURN-FROM (local + escaping), TAGBODY loops, CATCH/THROW,
UNWIND-PROTECT cleanup-on-throw, multiple values, PROGV; parity with the tree-walker
incl. the must-control / must-restart style cases.

DEPENDS ON: vm-4.

====================================================================
PROGRESS (v1.8.4, branch vm) — PART 1 of 2: lexical control done.

Lexical (non-escaping) control now compiles to byte-code JUMPs — no frames.  Since
compiled closures are vm-6, EVERY block/tag in a single compiled function is
non-escaping, so this is complete for the current VM:
- BLOCK / RETURN-FROM: forward JUMP to the block end; the exit value travels through
  a scratch local slot (allocated beyond the analyzer's n-locals) so the normal-fall
  and RETURN-FROM paths converge.  A RETURN-FROM taken from inside a sub-expression
  first CUTS the operand stack back to the block base (DROP, chunked to u8) — the VM
  is a stack machine, so the intervening partial values must be discarded.
- TAGBODY / GO: each tag is a label; GO cuts the stack to the tagbody base then JUMPs.
  TAGBODY returns NIL.  A codegen-time control-scope stack (in the compiler state)
  resolves RETURN-FROM/GO to the enclosing block/tag; escaping (via a closure) errors
  clearly (vm-6).
This lets the VM run real LOOPS: DOTIMES / DO / DOLIST (which macroexpand to
TAGBODY/GO/BLOCK) and early RETURN-FROM out of a loop all run and iterate without C
recursion.  Verified: tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_control (differential vs the
tree-walker, incl. a 1000-iteration countdown summing to 500500).  Full suite green.

REMAINING (PART 2 — dynamic NLX, next commit): CATCH/THROW, UNWIND-PROTECT, dynamic
(special) LET/LET*/PROGV binding save-restore, and MULTIPLE-VALUE-CALL / -PROG1
(hence MULTIPLE-VALUE-BIND/LIST, NTH-VALUE).  These need the setjmp/longjmp frame
machinery from eval.c (Frame + g_frame_stack; push_frame/pop_frame/find_catch_frame/
unwind_next_frame are static and must be exported) wired into the VM loop (new
opcode handlers BLOCK_OPEN/CATCH_OPEN/THROW/UNWIND_PROTECT/UNWIND_LEAVE/MV_*), plus
lifting the &optional-default/supplied-p/&key/&aux restriction with a prologue.  MV
codegen needs an mv-context threaded through %VM-GEN so single-valued tails set the
MultipleValues buffer.  (values) the FUNCTION already works via CALL-NAMED.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (v1.8.5, branch vm) — PART 2a: multiple values + non-capturing lambdas.

- MULTIPLE-VALUE-CALL / MULTIPLE-VALUE-PROG1 (hence MULTIPLE-VALUE-BIND / -LIST and
  NTH-VALUE, which macroexpand to them) now compile.  New opcode BC_MV_LIST
  (appended; BYTECODE_VERSION -> 2) replaces a producer's primary with the LIST of
  all its values from the MV buffer; codegen threads an mv-context (%VM-GEN-FOR-VALUES)
  so single-valued producer tails emit VALUES 1 to sync the buffer, then builds the
  call via the APPEND / APPLY (mv-call) and VALUES-LIST (mv-prog1) builtins.  Fixed
  BC_VALUES (it took the wrong element as primary for n>1).
- A NON-capturing LAMBDA in value position (:make-closure with no upvalue refs) now
  compiles to a standalone template pushed as a constant — covers FUNCALL/MAPCAR of a
  local lambda and the MULTIPLE-VALUE-BIND expansion's &optional/&rest lambda.  A
  capturing lambda (a depth>0 ref) still errors clearly (real closures = vm-6).
- Verified: tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_values (differential vs the tree-walker).

Known limitation (documented, uncommon): a BLOCK that returns MULTIPLE values via
(return-from b (values ...)) keeps only the primary — the block result travels
through a single scratch slot.  The common MV return (a function whose last form is
(values ...)) is unaffected (RETURN preserves the MV buffer).

REMAINING (PART 2b): CATCH/THROW, UNWIND-PROTECT, dynamic (special) LET/LET*/PROGV —
the setjmp/longjmp frame machinery wired into the VM loop.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 2b — DESIGN & HANDOFF (dynamic NLX: CATCH/THROW, UNWIND-PROTECT, special bind).

This is the setjmp/longjmp piece.  Research done (eval.c NLX map + GC audit); the
design below is ready to implement in a fresh session with full context budget.

FRAME MACHINERY (sources/eval.c, sources/kernel_types.h):
- `Frame` (kernel_types.h:140) has `next` (g_frame_stack link), `type` (frame_type:
  frame_catch/frame_protect/frame_dynamic_binding...), a `parameters` union
  (.catch.object = the tag), `MultipleValues* results`, and `jmp_buf non_local_exit`.
- `g_frame_stack` (eval.c:44) is non-static but not in a header — add
  `extern Frame* g_frame_stack;` (ideally to kernel_functions.h).
- CATCH/UNWIND-PROTECT/dynamic-bind are DYNAMIC (g_frame_stack only), NOT lexical, so
  the VM does NOT need eval.c's push_frame/pop_frame (those also link env->frames for
  the LEXICAL block/tagbody lookup, which the VM already handles as jumps — part 1).
  Write VM-local helpers that touch only g_frame_stack:
    vm_push_frame(f){ f->next=g_frame_stack; g_frame_stack=f; f->env_next=NULL; f->frame=NULL; }
    vm_pop_frame(){ Frame* r=g_frame_stack; g_frame_stack=r->next; return r; }
- EXPORT (un-static + declare) from eval.c: `find_catch_frame(tag, env)` (env unused,
  pass NIL) and `_Noreturn unwind_next_frame(popped, env)` (env unused).  The exit
  codes `non_local_exit_t` (0 initial / 1 unwind / 2 target) are in kernel.h.

GC ROOTING (audited): memory.c GC does NOT scan g_frame_stack.  Every Ref stored in a
VM frame (the catch tag; thrown values) MUST be add_to_live_pool'd (the VM's pool is
open for the whole bytecode_apply).  NOTE the operand-stack simple-vector roots ALL
its slots (even above sp) until overwritten, but a popped tag can be clobbered by a
later PUSH, so root it explicitly when storing into the frame.

VM-LOOP INTEGRATION (sources/bytecode.c, bytecode_apply):
- Keep a C-stack array `VmFrame vframes[N]` and a **volatile** counter `nvf`
  (volatile because it is read after longjmp to locate the active frame).  pc/sp need
  NOT be volatile IF the longjmp-return path only ASSIGNS them (never reads the stale
  value before assigning) — restore them from the frame's stored handler_pc/handler_sp.
  S/L/K/results/etc. are set once before any setjmp, so they are safe.
- Each frame-opening opcode does its own `setjmp(vframes[nvf].frame.non_local_exit)`
  (legal as `if (setjmp(...))`).  On return 0: fill the frame, vm_push_frame, nvf++,
  continue.  On return !=0: the frame being handled is g_frame_stack top == the
  vframe at vframes[nvf-1]; read handler_pc/handler_sp/kind from it.
    * code==2 (target, a CATCH hit): restore pc=handler_pc, sp=handler_sp, the thrown
      values are already in results (see THROW); PUSH the primary; vm_pop_frame; nvf--.
    * code==1 (unwind, passing through): run this frame's cleanup, vm_pop_frame, nvf--,
      then unwind_next_frame(&vf->frame, NIL) to continue the cascade (never returns).
      For a PROTECT frame the "cleanup" is bytecode: set pc=cleanup_pc and run it, and
      make BC_UNWIND_LEAVE finish the cascade (see below).  For a dynamic-binding frame
      the cleanup is C-only: restore the saved value cell, then unwind_next_frame.

OPCODES (all already reserved in bytecode.h; wire handlers + codegen):
- CATCH:  `gen tag; BC_CATCH_OPEN <off to L_handler>; <body>; BC_CATCH_CLOSE; JUMP L_end;
           L_handler: ; L_end:`  — CATCH_OPEN stores handler_pc=L_handler, catch base sp.
  THROW: `gen tag; gen-for-values value; BC_THROW` — value's MV are in results; find the
  catch via find_catch_frame(tag); copy *results into target->results; kick the cascade
  like eval.c's NON_LOCAL_EXIT: top=g_frame_stack; top->target=target;
  longjmp(top->non_local_exit, top==target?2:1).
- UNWIND-PROTECT: `BC_UNWIND_PROTECT <off to L_cleanup>; <protected>; BC_UNWIND_LEAVE(normal)
  ; L_cleanup: <cleanup for effect>; BC_UNWIND_END`.  Mirror eval.c:1145 — cleanup runs
  on BOTH paths: normal completion saves the protected value(s), runs cleanup, restores;
  an unwind runs cleanup then continues the cascade.  Track a per-protect "mode" so
  BC_UNWIND_END either resumes (normal) or calls unwind_next_frame (unwind).
- Dynamic (special) LET/LET*/PROGV: the analyzer already emits (:special name init)
  entries (LET) — lift %VM-GEN-LET's :special error.  Snapshot symbol_boundp/symbol_value,
  set_symbol_value(sym,new) (NB arg order is symbol,value — see kernel.c:1576), push a
  frame_dynamic_binding VmFrame carrying (sym, saved_boundp, saved_value); restore on
  both normal exit and unwind (set_symbol_value back, or symbol_make_unbound).  PROGV
  is the list-driven form of the same (:progv syms vals body).
- Also lift %VM-CHECK-CORE-LAMBDA-LIST's &optional-default/supplied-p/&key/&aux
  restriction by emitting a prologue (defaults for unsupplied optionals/keys need a
  supplied-p test; &aux is sequential LET; &key needs keyword matching — can reuse the
  arity pack's key bit + a keyword table in the constant pool).

Value APIs (public): symbol_value / set_symbol_value(sym,val) / symbol_boundp /
symbol_make_unbound; special_variable_p (variable.h).

ACCEPTANCE (unchanged, top of file): CATCH/THROW, UNWIND-PROTECT cleanup-on-throw,
dynamic bindings restored on normal + non-local exit, PROGV, and must-control style
cases; parity with the tree-walker.  Then this issue closes.
